TIER-3 PROOF FOR #1263 (mandatory, in YOUR #1263 worktree): (T3-a) THE EMIT: with the worktree's OWN `typescript` (after `npm ci` at Blockchain/Dev), `ts.transpileModule(<source>, { compilerOptions: { removeComments: true, target: <the package tsconfig's> } })` on the PARENT blob and on the HEAD blob -> the two outputs byte-identical (quote both byte counts and sha256; the seat: 10,805 bytes); (T3-b) ITS CONTROL: the same instrument on a copy with ONE code token changed (`toBeGreaterThan(1_000)` -> `(1_001)`) -> DIFFERENT, naming the line — an instrument that cannot say DIFFERENT proves nothing; (T3-c) every `-`/`+` line of `git diff -U0` parent..head is a comment line, with a control (a synthetic diff carrying one code line is flagged); (T3-d) the ks879 file at develop and at head (same cell count, all green) and the whole packages/shared suite at head == develop's count; (T3-e) ks879 on the END tree (it walks the other PRs' files). A DIFFERENT emit, or a code line in the diff = NO GO for #1263 (blocking). MAGIC-WORD-CLOSES is graded under the link hygiene (BY-NAME 8): say what must change before the squash (the body sentence re-worded and the Linear link set to `contributes`, or Wednesday rules KS-1140 closes).

THE READER RULE (the gate's, for every PR whose change READS text or output and turns it into a verdict — #1253's cell 9 over the subject's source and cell 7 over its own bytes; #1250's `sig_installable` over `trap -p` and `signal_run` over the runner's output and note). A shape counts as REAL when it occurs in the tree OR the PR's own commit / cell / body text NAMES it (as a shape the check catches OR as a shape it declares safe). A WRONG READING ON ANY REAL SHAPE = NO GO for that PR (blocking): name the shape, quote the line, describe the fix-shape and the regression cell. A wrong reading only on a shape that neither occurs nor is named is graded by REACH (non-blocking unless you show reach). Run every reading THROUGH THE REAL CODE PATH — never a lifted regex, never a Python port. FOR #1253 (Wednesday's commission, verbatim in substance): every real wrapped shape (`| cat`, `{ ...; } | cat`, backticks, a `(` line, `&`) must be flagged, and `false || bf` and `|| true` must NOT be flagged — through the REAL guard with SUBJ_SH=<copy>. Build each GATE-PROBE copy of the subject in YOUR worktree with ONE call site rewritten per shape at the c2 call site (`build_fixture "$WORK/c2" with-develop`, NOT c0 or c1: cells 10 and 11 use those as their own tamper anchors, and a shape there reds them for an anchor reason), ASSERT THE TAMPER APPLIED (the copy differs and the shape's line is present), run the guard with `SUBJ_SH=<copy>`, and quote cell 9's line from stdout. Shapes, in order: `| cat`, `{ …; } | cat`, backticks, a `(` on its own line, `&` + `wait` (each must be FLAGGED); `bf || true`, `false || bf` (each must NOT be flagged — both are named); then `if bf; then :; fi` and `bf && :` (named safe by the PR body's own table: must NOT be flagged); then `x=$(` / the call / `)` (the command-substitution class the round-1 commit names: must be FLAGGED), `( : noop` on the line above (must be FLAGGED: the code comment says the line above must not open a group), and `(` two lines above (the DECLARED boundary: grade as declared scope, by reach). For EVERY shape also measure the BEHAVIOUR through a subject copy run directly with an unbuildable fixture root, as gate24T2b did (`reader1253_2.out` there): does the `exit 2` abort reach the suite (rc 2, 1 cell) or is it swallowed (the suite runs its cells past a failed fixture)? A shape the guard flags must be one that swallows, and a shape it passes must be one that aborts — that is the whole reading. REACH: grep the subject and every other `*.test.sh` for each shape. The drafter's port and REAL-guard probe predict cell 9 WRONG on the four named-safe shapes and the multi-line `$(`: if the real guard confirms, THE READER RULE makes #1253 NO GO — AT THE CAP — say so plainly, with the reach and the cost of the fix, so Wednesday can weigh it; do not soften the rule yourself. If the real run reads them correctly, CONFIRMED for the seat — say why and correct the drafter.

#1250 UNDER RULING (a) (MANDATORY, cheap and decisive — run it FIRST). Two instruments, both in YOUR #1250 worktree only:
  (i) THE SUITE'S OWN ARMS, through the real suite with `RUNNER_SH=<a runner copy>` (the suite honours it: `RUNNER="${RUNNER_SH:-…}"`): (R-A) head suite + head runner, normal shell -> the seat: 58/0, "INT installable=yes", TERM and INT-group ASSERT — quote the three signal lines; (R-B) head suite in an INT-ignoring parent (`( trap '' INT; bash <suite> )`) -> the seat: 58/0, "INT installable=no", both INT arms UNREACHABLE naming their rule; (R-C) CONDITION (2): head suite + ROUND-1 runner (blob a01c9943c7a2…, `git show c78f4093fb53:…`) in a normal shell -> the seat: 56/2, the TERM arm and the INT-group arm RED with the swallow in their diagnostics — name both; (R-D) head suite + develop's runner (blob 85920863d704…) -> red on `dir_left=1` (the seat) — name every red; (R-E) INT-PID-PROBE: a GATE-PROBE copy of the head SUITE with ONLY the constant INT-to-pid `_skip` branch removed (so that arm runs `signal_run INT pid` like the others; assert the edit applied and nothing else changed: `diff` shows one hunk) -> run it with the head runner, the round-1 runner and develop's runner, normal shell: the drafter predicts OK / RED (rc=0 … verdict_lines=1) / RED. If the pid arm ASSERTS at head and REDS on round 1, the suite's constant UNREACHABLE for it is vacuously green and condition (1) is NOT MET for that arm (see RULING (a) above); if it cannot assert, the seat's rule is CONFIRMED — say which, from the output; (R-F) LATENCY: a runner copy with ONLY the background + `wait` reverted to the foreground call (`bash "$REPO_ROOT/$rel" > "$rss_suite_log" 2>&1`, `rss_rc=$?`) under the head suite -> do the three arms still pass? Time each arm. If they pass, no cell pins the latency the round-2 commit says the change exists for (lead LATENCY-UNPINNED, non-blocking unless you show reach); (R-G) the re-raise removed only (`kill -"$1" $$` -> `:`) in a runner copy -> the TERM arm must RED (it proves the TERM cell bites on the re-raise line itself).
  (ii) THE BLOCKING PROPERTY, a fixture probe (as gate24T2b's `probe_trap/trapprobe.py`): a throwaway tree beside your worktree with TWO fixture suites (a.test.sh starts a background GRANDCHILD `sleep 30 &` writing its pid to a file, then sleeps 4 s in the foreground; b.test.sh prints a marker), the runner under a TMPDIR > 80 chars, the signal sent after ~1.5 s by pid (or to the group), per runner (develop's, round 1's, round 2's) x arm: SIGTERM sent to the RUNNER's own pid (by pid, never by name); SIGINT to the pid with the driver under `set -m` (the suite's own shape); SIGINT to the pid from a plain `&` (no job control); SIGINT to the process group under `set -m` (a terminal ^C); and the hook-like pair (the driver holding SIGINT as SIG_IGN first): SIGTERM to the pid and SIGINT to the group. Record per run, stdout and stderr SEPARATELY: rc, whether the marker suite ran, the verdict line, whether the named /tmp/rss dir survives, whether suite a's own bash and its grandchild are alive 2 s after the signal, and any `No such file or directory`. THE BLOCKING RULE: after SIGTERM to the pid, the round-2 runner must exit NON-ZERO, run NO further suite, print NO `shell suites:` verdict and REMOVE the named /tmp/rss dir — any one missing = NO GO for #1250 (blocking), independent of the ruling. The INT rows are graded under RULING (a). ORPHAN-ON-SIGNAL is graded by reach against develop (the drafter measured develop's TERM leaving the WHOLE suite alive, round 2 leaving only the grandchild): a finding, not a blocker, unless you show a real suite whose orphan survives a gate run. Every /tmp/rss.* dir a develop run leaves is REPORTED by name, never removed (never delete; KS-1302 is the defect); every sleeper YOUR probe starts is ENDED BY PID.
  Then RUNNER-MASKS-INT and BG-STDIN: one fixture suite printing `( trap 'x' INT; trap -p INT )` and one reading stdin, under develop's runner and round 2's, normal shell, stdin from a pipe — quote both; census the 60 suites for any that trap INT for their OWN behaviour (not cleanup) or read stdin, and grade reach.

LESSONS CARRIED FROM gate24T2a AND gate24T2b (their reports, READ): (1) STREAM-SEPARATED captures — every run whose output a verdict PARSES (the runner output #1250's cells read, the TRAP probe, #1253's guard lines, jest `--json` for #1262) is captured with stdout and stderr in SEPARATE files, and you say which stream each quoted line came from; (2) PRESUITE-URLPATH — a red whose message carries `%20` / `%2520` or a path split at the space in "Testing Agent MAIN" is the environment class of the tester's path, pre-existing: confirm it identical at the parent, attribute it, never to a PR; (3) a KS-1155-class timeout — here #1250's KS-1303 `-lt 6` wall-clock cell reading >= 6 s, or a jest timeout in #1262's integration run on a COLD CACHE — gets ONE allowed re-run: report both runs with durations and `uptime`; an ASSERTION failure is REAL; (4) a tamper that does not apply is a FAIL of the arm, never a pass (assert every plant APPLIED before running it); (5) gate24T2b's own instrument slips (a path typo, `$PWD` read after `cd`) — absolute paths only.

